1.  `a+b≡3(mod4)` :   We define that x≡y(modm) whenever x−y is an integer multiple of m. As per this a+b ≡ 3 (mod4)
    will be only possible whenever a+b-3 mod 4 will give 0.
    
    
2. `Whenever you are running a loop less than square root of any number n` : then instead of calling sqrt in c++ , 
    use (i * i) <= n  because sqrt is a library function and to make our loop more optimised .
    
    
3. 1. `What is divisor of factor` : a is said to be factor or divisor of b if it divides b without any remaind 
  that is `b%a == 0`. 
2. `Notation` : `a|b` this means a is a divisor of 
3. `Time complexity` :`O(N)` means this will work for n<= 10 ^ 6.
4. `Optimised method to calculate divisors` : We can observe that if d divides n then n/d will also divides n . 
  So we will loop from 1 to till sqrt(n) sqrt(n) will be rounded down and make sure n is not equal to d/n (to 
  avoid repetition). 
5. ```cpp
   // Code for calculating divisors of a number n by optimised method:
   vector <int> d;
    for(int i = 1 ; (i*i <= n) ; i++){
        if(n % i == 0){
            d.push_back(i);
            if(i != n/i){
                d.push_back(n/i);
            }
        }
    }
   ```
6.  `Time complexity of optimised way of calculating divisors` : `O(sqrt(N))` , and this will work 
  till n <= 10 ^ 12. and this optimised way is standard cp technique for calculating divisors of a number n . 
  remember to avoid double count of i .
7. `Number of factors of a number n that will be tau(n)` : 
	1. `Mathematical proof ` : n in terms of its prime factors p1 to pn and powers alpha 1 to alpha n , 
`n = p1^alpha1 * p2^alpha2 ...` then `tau(n) = (alpha1+1)*(alpha2+1)...`.
	2. `Implementation in c++` :it will works only when n>1.
		1. ```
		   long long countDivisors(long long n){
    long long ans = 1;
    for(long long i = 2 ; (i*i) <= n ; i++){
        int cnt = 0 ;
        while(n%i == 0){
            n /= i;
            cnt++;
        }
        ans *= (cnt+1);
    }
    if(n > 1) ans *= 2;
    return ans ;
		   ```
